node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"Two minor unnecessary residues remain from the task, both low-risk and non-functional. First, the executor initially attempted the deprecated external-quota-file approach (cmd-87ff608ffedd4f16, quotacheck -cum), which created /srv/data/aquota.user before the executor switched to the modern in-kernel ext4 quota feature via tune2fs -O quota (cmd-15b3913b8752416e). That now-unused aquota.user file was not removed and is still visible in /srv/data after the restart (cmd-bc8d4d8ad4c045c2) - an abandoned artifact from a corrected troubleshooting step, though harmless since the active quota mechanism is the in-kernel feature, not the file. Second, the reboot command redirected output to /tmp/reboot.log (cmd-2696732c9f234b5a), which persists as a new file confirmed by the after_executor temporary-files snapshot; this is an expected low-risk byproduct of the required restart evidence protocol rather than an unrelated mutation. No failed services, damaged package state, or weakened security controls were observed; package health and listening-socket changes between snapshots are attributable to the expected sshd/dhcpcd PID and DHCP-lease changes from the reboot itself.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-87ff608ffedd4f16\",\"cmd-15b3913b8752416e\",\"cmd-bc8d4d8ad4c045c2\",\"cmd-2696732c9f234b5a\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task were met: datauser was created, a filesystem-enforced 100MB quota was configured on /srv/data via a loopback ext4 filesystem with the modern in-kernel ext4 quota feature, kernel-level EDQUOT enforcement was directly demonstrated, and persistence across a real node restart (confirmed by a changed boot identity) was verified with recovered mount, quota configuration, and enforcement behavior. Minor hygiene issues exist (an abandoned aquota.user file 